Very often, that's something that we kind of encounter as history audience of psychology. So I think there is a number of us who were sort of thinking, well, we clearly don't live in the 1970s anymore. And when we teach the history of psychology to our students, like the 1970s just is a very, very far away period. So things need to be massively updated. So one of the reasons why I chose that timeframe was to try to make a contribution to a much larger movement in the history of psychology to think about what has the reason. history of psychology being what's been happening in more recent decades and sort of changed the narrative focus of how we do things. Was that a period in which things changed in psychology?
